<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Razor">

	<PropertyGroup>
		<TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
		<ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
		<Nullable>enable</Nullable>
		<Version>5.4.51</Version>
		<!-- Brief current-version highlights only. Full per-version history with code samples lives in CHANGELOG.md (linked from the README). -->
		<!-- This is the text nuget.org shows on the listing page. It MUST lead with the version being shipped:
		     Directory.Build.targets (SDPKG001) fails the build when the leading version differs from <Version>.
		     It sat at "v5.4.16" through 5.4.29 because nothing checked it. -->
		<PackageReleaseNotes>v5.4.51 (forks 2.4.41) - NEW: accelerator.ReclaimableDeviceBytes(), the device memory the registered reclaimers (idle buffer pools) could give back now. v5.4.50 (forks 2.4.40) - NEW: Accelerator.DeviceMemoryBudgetBytes (an application budget for device memory) and Accelerator.AllocatedDeviceBytes; with a budget set, TryGetFreeMemory and the memory-pressure reclaim work on every backend, not only CUDA. NEW: AMD OpenCL reports free device memory. v5.4.49 (forks 2.4.39) - FIX (all backends): a kernel that fails to compile no longer breaks later kernels that call a helper it called (NullReferenceException); its helpers are removed from the context and generated again. v5.4.48 (forks 2.4.38) - FIX (WebGPU): 5.4.47 could leave a generator placeholder in the WGSL ("if (/*__UFIF0__*/) {") of a kernel that returns early on a condition read from a view and later calls a helper that takes a view; Chrome rejected the shader. A placeholder that is still there after generation is now a compile error. v5.4.47 (forks 2.4.38) - FIX (WebGPU): a barrier under a condition read from a view at a group-uniform address (if (n &lt; N) with N from a params view) compiles (Tint treats read_write storage loads as non-uniform; the condition is taken from invocation 0 through workgroupUniformLoad); FIX (WebGPU): no unreachable trailing return (Chrome "code is unreachable" warning) and no stray carriage returns in the generated WGSL. v5.4.46 (forks 2.4.38) - FIX (WebGPU): the same kernel compiled again gives byte-identical WGSL, so the compiled-shader cache and the browser pipeline cache hit (names no longer come from IR node ids); FIX (WebGL): byte-identical GLSL and the same GL program id for the same text (a recompiled kernel no longer links a duplicate program); FIX (all backends): a kernel optimizes the same whatever the context compiled before (IRContext.Optimize optimizes callees before their callers); FIX (all backends): a kernel that fails to compile no longer makes every later new kernel in the context fail (NullReferenceException in IfConversion); its half-built methods are removed. v5.4.45 (forks 2.4.37) - FIX (WebGPU): a dropped kernel is freed (the shader-resolve cache held every dispatched kernel); FIX (Wasm): a worker module-cache flush no longer strands an older live accelerator (Module not cached for kernelId N); NEW (Wasm, WebGL): the shader-artifact cache rebuilds a kernel loaded again (after its owner was collected or after ReleaseCompilerCaches) without IR or code generation; FIX: the artifact cache key includes the context code-generation settings. v5.4.44 (forks 2.4.37) - NEW: accelerator.ReleaseCompilerCaches() releases the context cache of your kernel code after a batch of loads (browser heap); FIX: Context.ClearCache / ReleaseCompilerCaches is thread-safe against kernel loads on other threads (a context-wide compile lock); FIX: the argument mapper type table is locked. v5.4.43 (forks 2.4.36) - FIX (WebGL): buffers are tiled to the device MAX_TEXTURE_SIZE (browsers without a GPU, SwiftShader 8192). v5.4.42 (forks 2.4.36) - FIX: IBrowserMemoryBuffer.CopyToHostUint8ArrayAsync returns an owned copy on Wasm and WebGL. v5.4.41 (forks 2.4.36) - NEW: ArrayView.CopyToHostUint8ArrayAsync(); FIX: MemoryBuffer readbacks accept the native buffer behind a view; Uint8Array readbacks are owned copies on Wasm and WebGL. v5.4.40 (forks 2.4.36) - FIX (browser): reading a view address as long (LoadEffectiveAddressAsPtr) in hot host code could abort the page .NET runtime or return a garbage high word (Mono wasm interpreter conv.i8 bug); the address is now integer arithmetic. v5.4.39 (forks 2.4.35) - FIX (Wasm): a sub-view device copy could throw with a garbage target offset in a busy page. v5.4.38 (forks 2.4.35) - FIX: Math/MathF.Log2, Cbrt, Asinh, Acosh, Atanh compile in kernels (musl libm ports; + IntrinsicMath.Log1P); FIX (WebGPU/WebGL): accurate f32 log/log2/log10; FIX (WebGPU): short-circuit branches meeting before the merge returned 0; PERF: ArgumentMapper no longer blows up browser AOT builds; FIX (WebGPU/WebGL): a call nothing implements fails to compile (was a silent 0). v5.4.37 (forks 2.4.34) - FIX (CUDA): AllocatePageLocked1D / CreatePageLockFromPinned bind the accelerator first (a page lock made off the accelerator's thread threw "invalid device context"); Wasm worker reuses kernel instances across dispatches. v5.4.36 (forks 2.4.33) - PERF (Wasm): a dispatch no longer copies every argument buffer into the linear memory and back out; buffers stay in place between dispatches and are written back only when something needs them (a 64-thread kernel over a 32 MB buffer: 12.5 -> 0.25 ms per launch). v5.4.35 (forks 2.4.33) - FIX (Wasm): disposing a buffer while work queued on it has not run yet no longer fails with RangeError: offset is out of bounds; the buffer is released after that work. v5.4.34 (forks 2.4.33) - FIX (WebGPU / WebGL): MathF.Sin / Cos / Tan accurate at any argument up to 102943 (were 2.7e-4 off at |x| = 2000; now within 1.2e-7 like CUDA / OpenCL / Wasm). PERF (CPU accelerator): a launch of a kernel with barriers or shared memory no longer wakes every runtime thread (about 15 ms per launch -&gt; 1.7 ms for one group). FIX (all backends but Wasm; wrong rounding): a double converted to Half / bf16 / FP8 / FP4 rounds once, to the nearest value of the type, as System.Half does (was rounded to float first). FIX (WebGPU / WebGL Dekker and Ozaki doubles): a double outside the f32 range loaded with a wrong exponent (1e-300 as 1.6e-38); WebGPU doubles below 2^-79 lost their low 29 bits; (float)-0.0 was +0; a double at the f32 overflow edge became NaN; Ozaki (float)d tied the wrong way just past an f32 midpoint. PlaywrightMultiTest honors a test's declared timeout. v5.4.33 (forks 2.4.32) - FIX (CUDA, OpenCL, WebGPU, WebGL, Wasm; silent wrong results): a conversion into bf16, FP8 E4M3 / E5M2 or FP4 E2M1 rounds to the type (was the identity: (float)(BFloat16)x returned x). FIX (same backends; silent wrong results): Half / bf16 / FP8 / FP4 arithmetic results round to the type as the .NET operators do (were float-exact until a store; up to 4,085 of 4,096 Half products differed). FIX (WebGPU): emulated-f16 float -&gt; Half rounds; native f16 division matches .NET. FIX (WebGL): FP8 / FP4 decoders keep -0. OpenCL: a kernel that fails to compile carries the build log in CLException. New tests on every backend: LowPrecisionRoundTrip_*, LowPrecisionArithmetic_*. See CHANGELOG.md.</PackageReleaseNotes>
		<GeneratePackageOnBuild>True</GeneratePackageOnBuild>
		<GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
		<EmbedAllSources>true</EmbedAllSources>
		<DebugType>Embedded</DebugType>
		<Title>SpawnDev.ILGPU</Title>
		<Authors>LostBeard</Authors>
		<Description>.Net GPU compute for the browser and desktop. Write once, run on WebGPU, WebGL, Wasm, CUDA, OpenCL, and CPU.</Description>
		<PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/LostBeard/SpawnDev.ILGPU</PackageProjectUrl>
		<PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
		<PackageLicenseFile>LICENSE.txt</PackageLicenseFile>
		<PackageIcon>icon-128.png</PackageIcon>
		<RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/LostBeard/SpawnDev.ILGPU.git</RepositoryUrl>
		<RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
		<PackageTags>Blazor;BlazorWebAssembly;Cuda;OpenCL;WebGPU;WebGL;WebAssembly;Multithreading;WebWorkers;GPGPU;ILGPU;ParallelProcessing;ComputeShader;CrossPlatform</PackageTags>
		<NoPackageAnalysis>true</NoPackageAnalysis>
		<EnableDefaultCompileItems>true</EnableDefaultCompileItems>
		<IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
		<LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
		<AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
		<!-- Trim safe, enforced: the trim analyzer runs on every build and any trim (IL2xxx) warning fails it. -->
		<IsTrimmable>true</IsTrimmable>
		<EnableAotAnalyzer>true</EnableAotAnalyzer>
		<WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);CS0184;IL2026;IL2046;IL2057;IL2060;IL2062;IL2065;IL2067;IL2069;IL2070;IL2072;IL2073;IL2074;IL2075;IL2076;IL2077;IL2078;IL2080;IL2087;IL2088;IL2089;IL2090;IL2091;IL2092;IL2093;IL2094;IL2095;IL2106;IL2111;IL2112;IL2113;IL2114</WarningsAsErrors>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<PackageReference Include="SpawnDev.SpawnJS" Version="3.0.8" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<!-- The unit-test project asserts internal invariants (e.g. the cache-key drift guard). -->
		<InternalsVisibleTo Include="SpawnDev.ILGPU.Demo.Shared" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<!-- =====================================================================
	     CRITICAL: FOUR-PACKAGE BUNDLE - read before bumping any one of these.
	     =====================================================================
	     A bug fixed under ILGPU/ ships in ILGPU.dll which lives in the SEPARATE
	     SpawnDev.ILGPU.Fork nupkg, NOT in this SpawnDev.ILGPU wrapper. If you
	     bump only this csproj, consumers transitively pull the OLD Fork from
	     nuget.org and the fix is invisible to them. To ship an ILGPU/ fix you
	     MUST bump all four together:

	       (1) ILGPU/ILGPU.csproj                                  <Version>
	       (2) ILGPU.Algorithms/ILGPU.Algorithms.csproj            <Version>
	       (3) SpawnDev.ILGPU/SpawnDev.ILGPU.csproj                <Version>
	       (4) The TWO PackageReference lines IMMEDIATELY BELOW
	           (the !Exists branch) - in-repo dev uses ProjectReference and
	           does not see these; the published nuspec dependency does.

	     Then run _check-fork-version-sync.bat at repo root to verify all four
	     match before commit. CI runs the same check on every push. If you skip
	     step (4) the consumer ships rc.N with stale Fork 2.0.x and the fix
	     never reaches the IR. This is exactly what bit rc.28 on 2026-04-28.
	     ===================================================================== -->
	<!-- Conditional fork reference: in-repo dev uses ProjectReference (fast iteration); external consumers use PackageReference via NuGet -->
	<ItemGroup Condition="Exists('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\ILGPU\ILGPU.csproj')">
		<ProjectReference Include="..\ILGPU\ILGPU.csproj" />
		<ProjectReference Include="..\ILGPU.Algorithms\ILGPU.Algorithms.csproj" />
	</ItemGroup>
	<ItemGroup Condition="!Exists('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\ILGPU\ILGPU.csproj')">
		<PackageReference Include="SpawnDev.ILGPU.Fork" Version="2.4.41" />
		<PackageReference Include="SpawnDev.ILGPU.Algorithms.Fork" Version="2.4.41" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<None Include="Assets\icon-128.png" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
		<None Include="..\LICENSE.txt" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
		<None Include="..\README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
		<None Include="wwwroot\icon-128.png" />
		<!-- The dispatch helper is ALSO embedded so its content hash can version the import URL (WebGPUDispatchPlan.HelperContentVersion). -->
		<EmbeddedResource Include="wwwroot\webgpuDispatchPlan.js" LogicalName="SpawnDev.ILGPU.webgpuDispatchPlan.js" />
		<!-- precompiled-shaders Layer 2: the build automation .targets is auto-imported by NuGet
		     from build/. Gated OFF by default (<SpawnDevPrecompileShaders>), so consumers that do
		     not opt in are unaffected. -->
		<None Include="build\SpawnDev.ILGPU.targets" Pack="true" PackagePath="build\" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<!-- precompiled-shaders Layer 2: bundle the precompiler worker (+ its dependency closure) into
	     the package tools/ folder so a consumer that opts in gets it with zero config. Gated on
	     PackPrecompilerTool (default FALSE) so the everyday build/pack stays fast - publishing the
	     tool is only done for an explicit release pack:
	         dotnet pack SpawnDev.ILGPU/SpawnDev.ILGPU.csproj -c Release -p:PackPrecompilerTool=true
	     When not bundled, the .targets emits a clear error pointing at <SpawnDevPrecompilerToolPath>. -->
	<PropertyGroup Condition="'$(PackPrecompilerTool)' == 'true'">
		<TargetsForTfmSpecificContentInPackage>$(TargetsForTfmSpecificContentInPackage);_BundlePrecompilerTool</TargetsForTfmSpecificContentInPackage>
	</PropertyGroup>
	<Target Name="_BundlePrecompilerTool">
		<Exec Command="dotnet publish &quot;$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\SpawnDev.ILGPU.Precompiler\SpawnDev.ILGPU.Precompiler.csproj&quot; -c $(Configuration) -o &quot;$(IntermediateOutputPath)precompiler-tool&quot;" />
		<ItemGroup>
			<_PrecompilerToolFiles Include="$(IntermediateOutputPath)precompiler-tool\**\*.*" />
			<TfmSpecificPackageFile Include="@(_PrecompilerToolFiles)">
				<PackagePath>tools\%(RecursiveDir)%(Filename)%(Extension)</PackagePath>
			</TfmSpecificPackageFile>
		</ItemGroup>
	</Target>

	<!-- =================================================================================================
	     THIS TARGET IS THE REASON _publish-nuget.bat USES `pack`. DO NOT "FIX" THAT TO `build`.
	     =================================================================================================
	     TargetsForTfmSpecificContentInPackage is a PACK-time extension point. Under
	     GeneratePackageOnBuild the target above still RUNS - it publishes the tool into
	     obj\...\precompiler-tool and the DLLs are sitting right there - but its files never reach the
	     package. MEASURED 2026-09-01, same property, same configuration:

	         dotnet build -c Release -p:PackPrecompilerTool=true  ->  1,529,731 bytes,  0 tools\ entries
	         dotnet pack  -c Release -p:PackPrecompilerTool=true  ->  6,659,041 bytes, 15 tools\ entries

	     SpawnDev.ILGPU/_publish-nuget.bat is the ONLY publish bat of 247 in the workspace that packs, which
	     reads like an agent's mistake and is not. Converting it to `build` ships a package with no
	     precompiler worker, silently - both verbs exit 0 and both write a correctly-versioned nupkg.
	     Hooking the target to _GetPackageFiles instead was also tried, and also produced 0 tools\ entries.

	     ⚠️ AND THE PAYLOAD IS ONLY REGENERATED WHEN THE VERSION CHANGES. Packaging is incremental:
	         GenerateNuspec: Skipping target "GenerateNuspec" because all output files are up-to-date
	     Re-packing an already-packed version yields the 1.5 MB tool-less package, and deleting
	     bin\Release\*.nupkg AND obj\Release\*.nuspec does NOT change that - only an unseen version does.
	     That is why this works in practice (a release bumps the version) and why re-publishing the same
	     version would quietly not. The bat verifies the artifact and aborts on an empty tools\ rather than
	     trusting the exit code.

	     ⚠️ When it does run fully the glob OVER-collects: the Precompiler's publish drags in this project's
	     own WebGPU\, WebGL\ and Wasm\ asset folders (72 DLLs / 105 MB uncompressed -> a 47.7 MB nupkg).
	     6.6 MB is the intended shape, so narrow the glob before changing the invalidation behaviour.

	     PROPER FIX, for whoever has the time: SpawnDev.SpawnJS.WebWorkers bundles its rollup tooling with a
	     STATIC <Content Include="tasks\**" Pack="true" PackagePath="tasks"> over PRE-BUILT, COMMITTED
	     files. Static items exist at project evaluation, so GeneratePackageOnBuild packs them and no pack
	     step is needed at all. Adopting that here means deciding how the precompiler's output is produced
	     and whether it gets committed - a real design call, not a mechanical swap. See
	     _DevComms/2026-09-01-tuvok-TO-geordi-ilgpu-publish-bat-is-the-last-pack-based-one.md -->


	<ItemGroup>
		<Content Remove="wwwroot\icon-128.png" />
		<!-- Wasm/repro/ is tracked debugging-repro scratch (V8 barrier bug repros, .wasm/.wat/.json
		     fixtures). The Razor SDK auto-sweeps its *.json (manifest/stats/sitemap) into the package
		     as content/ + contentFiles/ - junk that has no business in the shipped library. Exclude
		     the whole repro tree from packing (it is not a build input). -->
		<Content Remove="Wasm\repro\**" />
		<None Remove="Wasm\repro\**" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<SupportedPlatform Include="browser" />
		<SupportedPlatform Include="windows" />
		<SupportedPlatform Include="linux" />
		<SupportedPlatform Include="macos" />
	</ItemGroup>

</Project>
